  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 514 AM EDT SAT SEP 26 2026\n\nThe start of this period is characterized by clear skies and \nquiet conditions with surface and upper level high pressure\ndominating. Ridge/valley splits Sunday Night still look likely \nwith lows in the mid 40s in the valleys and low 50s on the ridge \ntops and some valley fog is also probable. Looking at the \nnationwide picture, an omega block will be setting up with two \nupper level lows/troughs near the east and west coasts and \nridging/high pressure in the central US. This puts eastern KY in \nthe eastern periphery of the ridging that will migrate east \nearly next week. This will extend the dry and quiet weather until \nPoPs return mid to late next week. As the ridge moves eastward we \nwill see a return of west to southwesterly flow allowing for a \ngradual warming trend starting on Monday. This warming will \ncontinue, with highs increasing each day until the upper 70s to\nmid 80s return around Wednesday. That west to southwesterly flow \nwill also allow for increased moisture advection into the OH/TN \nValleys, increasing humidity in the latter half of next week. \n\nUnsettled weather is set to return with rain chances starting around \nThursday. As the previously mentioned ridging moves through and \neventually out of the area, the initial western upper level low \nwill start to advance eastward as well. The trough is progged to \nslow down quite a bit in the central US with a southeast ridge \nlikely being the culprit. Uncertainty exists in the evolution of \nthis pattern and the subsequent rain chances for the region next \nweek. But, the solutions that seems most plausible at this time \nis that as the core of the upper level slows down in the central \nUS, some subtle shortwaves could eject and move downwind following\nthe contour of the southeast ridge. This would put those \nshortwaves somewhere in the region starting in the mid-late next \nweek timeframe. NBM and LREF show gradually increasing PoPs with a\n50-55% chance of >.01\" of rain by Friday. It is important to note\nthat considerable uncertainty exists going into next weekend, but\nNBM and LREF do show PoPs increasing to 70-80% of >.01\" of rain \nbeyond the end of the 7 day period by later Sunday. \n\nAll in all, we are still on track to see temps increasing daily \nstarting next week, with upper 70s to 80s returning around \nWednesday. Low rain chances return as well starting around \nThursday and then increase as we go into next weekend. The usual \nmodel uncertainties with a forecast this far out makes finer \ndetails about next week's rain chances hard to determine at this \ntime.",
